# Vimla Real Data & Visual Reference Rules

These rules are mandatory for all production UI work.

## 1. Reference hierarchy

When visual references disagree, use this priority:

1. security and product/domain correctness;
2. existing backend/API/contracts and persisted state;
3. `docs/DESIGN_SYSTEM.md` and `@vimla/ui`;
4. `docs/design/references/00-master-design-system.png`;
5. screen-specific references;
6. responsive rules.

The master reference owns the canonical visual language.

Screen-specific references own composition for their screen, not a separate design system.

## 2. One navigation system

Do not create different sidebar/navigation designs per screenshot.

Use one shared `AppShell` / `Sidebar` / mobile navigation system.

If a screen reference shows a different sidebar:
- preserve the canonical sidebar visual language;
- use the screen reference only for the main content composition;
- change navigation only when the actual product information architecture requires it.

## 3. No hardcoded production data

Production UI must not hardcode user/business/domain data.

Do NOT hardcode:
- names;
- emails;
- avatars;
- project titles;
- participant lists;
- messages;
- plan prices;
- plan limits;
- AI usage;
- storage usage;
- payment history;
- card/payment details;
- payment statuses;
- dates;
- counts;
- model catalog entries;
- Project Brain facts;
- decisions;
- actions;
- files;
- finance metrics.

These values must come from real:
- API responses;
- contracts;
- authenticated session state;
- database-backed domain state;
- versioned policies;
- server configuration;
- feature flags.

## 4. What may be static

Static code is acceptable for:
- design tokens;
- component variants;
- route definitions;
- stable enum-to-label/icon mappings;
- localization keys;
- documented product constants that are truly code-level constants.

Do not confuse a design example with a product constant.

## 5. Missing features shown in references

If a screenshot contains a feature that is not implemented:

Do NOT:
- add fake production cards;
- add local arrays that pretend to be server data;
- add a clickable fake route;
- add fake billing/project/AI state;
- silently invent backend behavior.

Instead choose one of these:
1. omit the feature from production UI for now;
2. expose only a disabled/coming-soon state if the product specification explicitly requires it;
3. implement the missing domain/backend/contracts/persistence first in a separately scoped feature task.

Visual-only examples are allowed in:
- local/test-only `/dev/ui`;
- test fixtures;
- Storybook-like local catalogs if later introduced.

They must never leak into production runtime paths.

## 6. Existing features must use real data

When restyling existing screens, preserve current data flows.

Examples:
- Auth uses Better Auth/current auth APIs.
- Usage uses `/v1/usage` or current equivalent.
- Plans use the real plan catalog/API.
- Payments use real payment APIs/history.
- AI models use the real model catalog.
- Admin finance uses the existing FinanceQueryService/Admin API.
- Sessions use existing session APIs.

Do not replace live data with mock data simply to match a screenshot.

## 7. Loading, empty, and error states

If real data is not available yet:
- show `Skeleton` while loading;
- show `EmptyState` for real empty results;
- show `ErrorState` for failures.

Do not fill empty states with fake records.

## 8. No UI-only domain truth

Frontend must not become the source of truth for:
- pricing;
- limits;
- entitlements;
- permissions;
- billing math;
- Usage grants;
- payment methods;
- finance calculations;
- project limits.

The frontend renders authoritative backend/domain results.

## 9. Strict API evolution

If a design requires data that the current API does not provide:
- update contracts deliberately;
- implement backend/domain support;
- add tests;
- then render it.

Do not derive sensitive/financial truth heuristically in the browser.

## 10. Production route integrity

Navigation items must correspond to real routes and real feature availability.

Do not show a production navigation item merely because it appears in a visual reference.

Feature availability must be driven by actual implementation and permissions.

## 11. Test requirement

Tests must catch accidental mock/hardcoded production data where practical.

For critical screens, verify rendered values are driven by fixture/API responses rather than literal screenshot values.

## 12. Main rule

Visual references define appearance and composition.

The backend/domain defines truth.

Never sacrifice real data integrity to make the UI look more like a generated mockup.
